cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use TrustLab in your work, please cite it as below."
title: "TrustLab: Measuring the False-Confidence Gap and a Calibrated Trust Metric for AI-Driven Software Testing"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Rollamadugu"
    given-names: "Krishnasai"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0001-9992-8997"
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-10-20"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/rollamadugu/trustlab"
keywords:
  - software testing
  - test automation
  - mutation testing
  - test oracle
  - self-healing tests
  - LLM-as-judge
  - trustworthy AI
  - calibration
abstract: >-
  TrustLab is a reproducible research prototype that measures when AI-generated,
  AI-healed, and AI-adjudicated test verdicts can be believed. It defines the
  False-Confidence Gap (the distance between how often tests appear to pass and
  how often they are actually sound), grounds soundness in real AST-based
  mutation testing, and builds a calibrated trust metric evaluated with ROC-AUC,
  Mann-Whitney U, Vargha-Delaney A12, and Expected Calibration Error against a
  naive baseline across three AI testing modalities.
